# The ikigai-ledger vocabulary.
#
# Self-contained under `https://ikigai-rs.dev/ns/ledger#` — the `sig:` and `log:`
# precedent (ikigai-sign, ikigai-log) — so no arc here blocks on a manual
# /ns deploy, and a domain this size stays out of the kernel's vocabulary. A term is
# promoted into the shared `vocabulary.ttl` when it earns a second consumer.
#
# ★ THE ONE TERM THAT SHOULD PROBABLY NOT LIVE HERE: `ledger:about`. It is the join
# between an item and the FILE, COMMIT, PR or BRIEF it is about, and its whole value is
# that everything filed against a resource answers one query. `ikigai-browse` already
# has the same edge under a different name — `ik:annotates`, in the kernel's own
# vocabulary, flattening `oa:hasTarget` away — so today "everything filed against this
# file" is a UNION of two predicates that mean the same thing. The right fix is a
# general `ik:about` in `ikigai-vocab` with `ik:annotates` and this term as
# sub-properties; that is a core change, so it is REPORTED rather than reached, and
# migrating this predicate later is one SPARQL UPDATE over one graph.
#
# Reused rather than reinvented, so a consumer's existing queries work:
#   dcterms:title      an item's title
#   dcterms:created    when it was filed
#   dcterms:modified   when it last changed
#   prov:Entity        what an item and a comment ARE
#   prov:Activity      what a selection IS (something that ran, at a time)
#   prov:generatedAtTime / prov:invalidatedAtTime  when it ran / when it died
#   sig:contentHash    the tagged digest convention (ikigai-sign's term, so a
#                      tombstone's hash JOINS with a signature graph instead of
#                      colliding with it)
#
# This file IS the published document: it is served at
# https://ikigai-rs.dev/ns/ledger, deployed by hand, and checked against this repo's
# main by ikigai-devtools' `ns-drift`, which reads the `owl:versionInfo` below on both
# sides. That line is the crate version (test-pinned), so redeploy /ns/ledger after every
# release that changes this file, or the live copy claims the previous one.

@prefix ledger: <https://ikigai-rs.dev/ns/ledger#> .
@prefix rdf:    <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:   <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix prov:   <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix xsd:    <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix sig:    <https://ikigai-rs.dev/ns/sign#> .
@prefix owl:    <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .

<https://ikigai-rs.dev/ns/ledger> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "ikigai-ledger" ;
    rdfs:comment "The work ledger's terms: items, comments, labels, links, claims, and the events that say who claimed, released, handed back and edited each item. 0.15.0 adds ledger:ClaimEvent, ledger:ReleaseEvent, ledger:EditEvent, ledger:takeoverFrom, ledger:releaseReason, ledger:handedBackBy and ledger:editKind, drops rdfs:domain from ledger:claimedBy, ledger:claimedAt, ledger:claimKind and ledger:claimPrincipal (an event carries them too), and lets ledger:closedAtSource say import (ledger #2819). It also adds ledger:AliasScheme, ledger:Alias, ledger:schemeName, ledger:schemeOwner, ledger:aliasScheme, ledger:aliasValue and ledger:aliasOf: an item resolvable by an external id, in a scheme one principal owns (ledger #2828). And 0.17.0 adds ledger:Link, ledger:Mirror, ledger:linkType, ledger:linkTarget, ledger:mirrorOf, ledger:subjectOpen, ledger:linkPresent and ledger:mirrorVersion: typed links across ledgers (ledger #2829). And 0.18.0 adds ledger:stateFrom, ledger:stateTo, ledger:editLabel, ledger:editTarget, ledger:editLinkType and ledger:editField: an edit event names what it changed (ledger #2841); ledger:evidence: what closed an item, on that close's StatusEvent, beside its ledger:closedReason, which therefore drops its rdfs:domain (ledger #2842); ledger:eventKey: an imported event's id at its source, on claim, release, edit and status events an import principal records with claimed dates, which change nothing on the item (ledger #2843); ledger:movesTo: the moves a lifecycle allows from each state, which the state Sink enforces when any are declared (ledger #2863); ledger:lifecycleVersion: the version of the lifecycle a state event was checked against, read from the document (ledger #2864); ledger:claimedModified: the dcterms:modified an import principal last claimed, which the claim holds while dcterms:modified equals (ledger #2932)." ;
    owl:versionInfo "0.18.1" .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# The spine
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

ledger:Item a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf prov:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Item" ;
    rdfs:comment "One unit of work in the ledger. Skolemized as urn:iki:ledger:{ledger}:item:{id}, where {ledger} names the partition the item belongs to and {id} is opaque and time-ordered; the human-facing `#12` is ledger:number, a LABEL on that IRI and never the identifier. A prov:Entity because it is a thing that exists over time and is revised, not an occurrent. ★ THE BASE CLASS OF A TYPED FAMILY: every item asserts rdf:type ledger:Item, and an item may ALSO assert a more specific class (rdfs:subClassOf ledger:Item) for its level — a decision record, a review finding, a state transition. Different levels really do have different scope, responsibility and granularity, so they are different CLASSES with their own shapes; what they share is the plumbing — one identity scheme, five verbs, one manifold, one capability model, one set of golden threads, one query surface, one selection surface. The base type is asserted on every item DELIBERATELY, so \"everything in the ledger\" is one triple pattern and needs no reasoner, and a new level is additive: a class IRI, a shape, and a `kind=` argument value. Nothing in this module enumerates the subclasses." .

ledger:Comment a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf prov:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Comment" ;
    rdfs:comment "A note appended to an item, stamped and attributed, never edited in place. Skolemized as urn:iki:ledger:{ledger}:comment:{id}." .

ledger:Tombstone a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf prov:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Tombstone" ;
    rdfs:comment "The evidence a deleted item leaves behind: which number existed, when it was removed, by whom, why, how many quads went, and the sha256 of exactly what went. A ledger that silently forgets is not a ledger, so nothing leaves without one — including a purge, where the tombstone is all that remains. Skolemized as urn:iki:ledger:{ledger}:tombstone:{id}, keeping the deleted item's id so the two are joinable." .

ledger:Counter a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Counter" ;
    rdfs:comment "The short-id allocator, as a resource in the graph rather than as process state: urn:iki:ledger:{ledger}:counter carries ledger:lastNumber. It is read at every append and never decremented, so a restart continues where the last writer stopped and a purge cannot make a number be reused. ★ ONE PER LEDGER, because the counter lives in the ledger's own named graph: numbers restart at 1 in each partition, which is why a display number carries the ledger's name (acme#12) once there is more than the default one." .

ledger:Ledger a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Ledger" ;
    rdfs:comment "One named partition: its own named graph (urn:iki:ledger:graph:{name}), its own counter, its own capabilities (urn:cap:ledger:{read,write,delete,purge}:{name}), and its own IRI family (urn:iki:ledger:{name}:*). ★ A NAME, NOT A TAG: a label is data and a capability binds to a resource name, so a tag partition can only ever be a view while a named one can be a boundary. The bare urn:iki:ledger:* forms address the ledger called `default` through the same grammar match and therefore the same capability — sugar, not a second door. ⚠ A ledger and a LAYER are the same mechanism (a named graph plus a capability) on different axes — partition vs participant — and a graph that is trying to be both is a graph nobody can reason about." .

ledger:Policy a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Policy" ;
    rdfs:comment "An ordering policy for urn:iki:ledger:next: what it weighs and in what order, resolvable at urn:iki:ledger:policy:{name}. The READY SET is a query and needs no policy; a policy only orders what is already ready." .

ledger:Selection a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf prov:Activity ;
    rdfs:label "Selection" ;
    rdfs:comment "One run of urn:iki:ledger:next: which policy ran, over how many candidates, what it ranked, and what it excluded and why. A prov:Activity because it is an occurrent — the answer is only true of the moment it was computed. ⚠ A selection OFFERS; it never authorizes. Acting on it still requires the actor's own capability." .

ledger:Ranking a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Ranking" ;
    rdfs:comment "One ranked candidate within a ledger:Selection: its position, the item, the score the policy gave it, and the reasons in the policy's own words. Skolemized as {selection}:rank:{n}." .

ledger:Exclusion a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Exclusion" ;
    rdfs:comment "One open item the ready-set query REFUSED to offer, and the deterministic reason (blocked, claimed, deferred, filtered). Recorded because \"why not that one\" is the question a selection gets asked, and an answer that cannot say is overridden and then ignored. Skolemized as {selection}:excluded:{n}." .

ledger:Status a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Status" ;
    rdfs:comment "The state of an item. An IRI rather than a string, so the values are a closed, dereferenceable set that a mapping from a relational source can target directly (kata's CHECK-constrained enum is exactly this, waiting for an IRI)." .

ledger:CloseReason a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "CloseReason" ;
    rdfs:comment "Why an item was closed. An IRI, for the same reason as ledger:Status." .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# The closed value sets
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

ledger:open a ledger:Status ;
    rdfs:label "open" ;
    rdfs:comment "Filed and not finished. Eligible for the ready set unless claimed, deferred or blocked." .

ledger:closed a ledger:Status ;
    rdfs:label "closed" ;
    rdfs:comment "Finished, with a ledger:closedReason. Closing is NOT deleting: a closed item is still in the graph, still queryable, and still a blocker that no longer blocks." .

ledger:done a ledger:CloseReason ;
    rdfs:label "done" ;
    rdfs:comment "The work was done." .

ledger:wontfix a ledger:CloseReason ;
    rdfs:label "wontfix" ;
    rdfs:comment "A decision not to do it. The decision is the value; the note that explains it is a comment." .

ledger:duplicate a ledger:CloseReason ;
    rdfs:label "duplicate" ;
    rdfs:comment "Another item covers it. Name the survivor as ledger:evidence on the close (since 0.18.0, ledger #2842), which needs no note, about or related beside it." .

ledger:superseded a ledger:CloseReason ;
    rdfs:label "superseded" ;
    rdfs:comment "Overtaken by later work or a later decision, which ledger:evidence on the close can name (since 0.18.0, ledger #2842)." .

ledger:auditNoChange a ledger:CloseReason ;
    rdfs:label "audit-no-change" ;
    rdfs:comment "Investigated, and the correct outcome was to change nothing. Taken from kata, which had it for the same reason our review arcs keep needing it: \"looked, found it fine\" is a real verdict and recording it as `done` loses the finding." .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Item properties
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

ledger:number a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "number" ;
    rdfs:comment "The human-facing short id — `#12`. A LABEL on a stable IRI, never the identifier: it is allocated from ledger:lastNumber, is unique per LEDGER (numbers restart in each, which is why a display number outside the default ledger carries the name, acme#12), and is never reused, because the counter is not lowered by a delete or a purge. ⚠ No domain: an item AND the tombstone it leaves both carry it, and rdfs:domain ledger:Item would make every tombstone an inferred item to a reasoning consumer — a deleted item counted as a live one." .

ledger:body a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "body" ;
    rdfs:comment "The prose of an item or a comment. An item's title is dcterms:title and its body is everything after the first blank line of what was filed." .

ledger:status a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range ledger:Status ;
    rdfs:label "status" ;
    rdfs:comment "An item's current status, or a StatusEvent's resulting status. No domain: a dated event must not become an inferred Item." .

ledger:closedReason a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range ledger:CloseReason ;
    rdfs:label "closedReason" ;
    rdfs:comment "An item's verdict while it is closed, removed on reopen. Since 0.18.0 also on the close's StatusEvent, where it stays (ledger #2842): the role of a ledger:evidence is read from it. No domain: a dated event must not become an inferred Item. A close written before 0.18.0 has none on its event." .

ledger:priority a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "priority" ;
    rdfs:comment "0 highest … 4 lowest, kata's convention kept deliberately so behavior can be diffed against the tool being replaced. ABSENT means unset, and absent is not 4: the default policy ranks any item with a priority above every item without one, which is only meaningful if \"unset\" is a distinct state." .

ledger:deferred a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "deferred" ;
    rdfs:comment "Not now — open, not blocked by anything, not claimed, and still deliberately not offered. Modeled as a property because kata's equivalent (`someday`) is a flag inside a metadata JSON blob that exactly one query understands, which is a real concept wearing a hiding place. ⚠ Deliberately a BOOLEAN and not a deferred-until date: readiness that turns on the wall clock would go stale in the cache with no golden thread to cut it, so resuming is an act (Delete urn:iki:ledger:defer) rather than the passage of time." .

ledger:about a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Resource ;
    rdfs:label "about" ;
    rdfs:comment "The resource this item is ABOUT — a file, a commit, a PR, a brief, a repo, an endpoint. The point of the ledger being RDF: \"what is open against this file\" is a query, not an index someone maintains, and it joins items to the five other node types (commits, PRs, reviews, annotations, log entries) that already carry resource IRIs. ⚠ See the header: ik:annotates is the same edge under another name, and the general term belongs in the kernel's vocabulary." .

ledger:revision a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "revision" ;
    rdfs:comment "The revision the item was filed against — a commit sha, a tag, a version. The commonest way a review ledger rots is a finding about line 40 of a file that has since moved, and this is the pin. One per item rather than one per ledger:about target, deliberately: it states what the tree looked like when the item was FILED, which stays unambiguous when an item is about several resources. Where the target IRI can carry its own revision, prefer that and this is the coarse stamp." .

ledger:raised a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:range xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "raised" ;
    rdfs:comment "When the claim an item records AROSE: the review, the audit, the day it was first noticed. Distinct from dcterms:created, which is when the item was FILED, and the two differ most exactly where the question \"what is oldest\" matters: a migration files hundreds of items in minutes, so ordering by dcterms:created sorts by migration order. OPTIONAL and set at append (`append raised=YYYY-MM-DD`); an item without it was raised when it was filed, so the honest ordering is ORDER BY COALESCE(?raised, ?created). A DATE and not a dateTime because that is the precision the sources carry (\"from the 2026-07-21 review\"). ⚠ ikigai-store (oxigraph) compares the two as points in time; SPARQL 1.1's operator table does not define comparing an xsd:date with an xsd:dateTime, so the portable spelling is COALESCE(STR(?raised), STR(?created)), whose lexical forms sort correctly as strings. Chosen over routing ledger:about to a source resource with its own date because the items that need it most came from markdown bullets, which are not resolvable resources with dates (ledger #240)." .

ledger:key a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "key" ;
    rdfs:comment "The CALLER'S name for an item: unique per ledger, chosen by whoever files it (`append key=`), and the thing a keyed append checks before it files. Its first use is idempotence (a hook re-run, two hooks racing) and its second is lookup by a foreign id (`urn:iki:ledger:item:key:{key}`, `items key=`) — a roborev finding, a kata issue — which is why it is called a key and not an idempotency key. A literal, not an IRI, so any caller's identifier fits; its shape is fixed (ASCII letters, digits, `-`, `.`, `_`, `~` and `:`, at most 256 characters) so it stays a legal IRI segment and survives a URL path. Compared exactly; never rewritten after filing. ⚠ No domain, for the ledger:number reason: an item AND the tombstone it leaves both carry it, so a deleted item's key stays TAKEN — a replayed request must not resurrect what someone deliberately deleted — and rdfs:domain ledger:Item would make every tombstone an inferred item to a reasoning consumer. ⚠ A key is a NAME, kept on the tombstone through a purge like the number is: it is not content, so do not put content in it." .

ledger:eventKey a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "event key" ;
    rdfs:comment "An imported event's id at its source (ledger #2843), and the mark of a history-only event: the subject the importer's batch named it by, unique per ledger and checked in the same store update that writes the event, so a re-sent event is recorded once. The same shape as ledger:commentKey, and like it not kept on a tombstone: deleting an item takes its events, keys and all." .

ledger:commentKey a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Comment ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "comment key" ;
    rdfs:comment "The CALLER'S name for a comment: unique per ledger, chosen by whoever writes it (`comment key=`), and checked in the same store update that writes the comment, so a replayed request (a hand-back seen twice, a hook re-run) comments once and answers the comment already there, marked existing. The same shape as ledger:key (ASCII letters, digits, `-`, `.`, `_`, `~` and `:`, at most 256 characters) and compared exactly. ⚠ A separate property from ledger:key on purpose: a keyed append refuses to file while ANY subject carries its ledger:key, so sharing the property would let a comment's key block an item's. ⚠ Unlike an item's key it is not kept on a tombstone: deleting an item moves its comments, keys and all, to the graveyard." .

ledger:label a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "label" ;
    rdfs:comment "A free tag. Filtering by label — must have ALL of these, must have NONE of those — belongs to the READY QUERY, not to an ordering policy; that is why kata's ranker can be six lines." .

ledger:author a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "author" ;
    rdfs:comment "Who an item, a comment or a tombstone is credited to. ★ Since 0.15.0 (ledger #2827) a write whose capability carries a principal records the author as that principal's IRI or one it was admitted to claim, and ledger:authorSource says how it was admitted: the committer itself (verified), a principal the committer acts for (claimed), or a foreign actor an import principal names (claimed). With no author argument the committer is the author. ⚠ A plain string with no ledger:authorSource is the older free-text form, and still what a write without a principal (root, a host whose door mints none) records: a claim nobody checked. prov:wasAttributedTo is still not used, because a free-text author cannot be its object." .

ledger:committer a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "committer" ;
    rdfs:comment "The principal that made the write: the one urn:cap:principal:<iri> the writer's capability carried, minted by the host's door (gonk names each QUIC client urn:iki:gonk:client:<fingerprint>; a participant is urn:collab:participant:{name}). Never an argument, so a caller cannot supply it. Stamped on every item, comment and tombstone a write creates, with ledger:committedAt (ledger #2827), and since 0.15.0 on every StatusEvent, ClaimEvent, ReleaseEvent and EditEvent, so every write has a who (ledger #2819). Absent when the capability carried no principal: root, or a host whose door mints none." .

ledger:committedAt a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "committed at" ;
    rdfs:comment "When the write that created this node landed, from the kernel clock and never from an argument. Equal to dcterms:created unless an import principal claimed an authored created date (ledger:createdSource import)." .

ledger:authorSource a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "author source" ;
    rdfs:comment "How ledger:author was admitted, as a literal in the ledger:closedAtSource shape: committer (the author IS the committer, so verified), delegate (a principal the committer acts for by prov:actedOnBehalfOf in the host's participants graph; claimed by the committer) or import (a foreign actor named by an import or sync principal, a ledger:Importer; claimed). Absent on a free-text author (see ledger:author)." .

ledger:createdSource a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "created source" ;
    rdfs:comment "The literal import when dcterms:created on an item or a comment is an authored date an import principal claimed, not the kernel time of the write (that is ledger:committedAt). Absent when the date is the kernel's. On an edit event it also marks a link or a state move an import principal dated at its source with created= (ledger #2934): that event changed its item like any other. A claim, release, edit or status event an import principal recorded afterward as history only (ledger #2843) carries it too, and is told apart by its ledger:eventKey: such an event changed nothing on its item, carries no ledger:eventSequence, and is not counted in a mirror's ledger:mirrorVersion." .

ledger:modifiedSource a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "modified source" ;
    rdfs:comment "The literal import when dcterms:modified on an item is an authored date an import principal claimed: when filing it, or since 0.18.0 by a later write it dated at the source (a comment's created, a close's closed-at, ledger #2928), which moves dcterms:modified only forward. Ranking reads dcterms:modified either way, so imported items tie-break as they did at their source. A write that carries no claimed date stamps dcterms:modified from the kernel clock and leaves this mark in place, so the claim holds exactly while dcterms:modified still equals ledger:claimedModified (ledger #2932); on an item written before 0.18.0, which has no ledger:claimedModified, it holds while dcterms:modified is earlier than ledger:committedAt, and the faces read it that way." .

ledger:claimedModified a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "claimed modified" ;
    rdfs:comment "The dcterms:modified an import principal last claimed on an item, beside ledger:modifiedSource, written by the filing or the later dated write that set it (0.18.0, ledger #2932). The claim holds while dcterms:modified still equals it: an undated write stamps the kernel's time over dcterms:modified and leaves this as the record of the claim it ended. Until 0.18.0 the read compared dcterms:modified with ledger:committedAt instead, so a date claimed after the filing's commit read as not claimed." .

ledger:Importer a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Importer" ;
    rdfs:comment "An import or sync principal: a principal the host's participants graph types ledger:Importer. Read, never written, by this module. Only an Importer may name a foreign actor as an author, claim an authored dcterms:created, dcterms:modified or ledger:closedAt (ledger #2827), or record past-dated history events (ledger #2843). Which principals are importers is enrollment data, not a capability." .

ledger:claimedBy a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "claimedBy" ;
    rdfs:comment "On an item, who holds it; on a ledger:ClaimEvent, the holder that took the claim; on a ledger:ReleaseEvent, the holder whose claim was released. The holder's free-text label, which tells apart two workers that share one principal. No domain since 0.15.0: an event must not become an inferred Item. On an item, the ready set never offers a claimed item, which is most of what \"what should I do next\" means when more than one worker shares a ledger. A claim may carry a lease (ledger:lease, ledger:leaseExpires) and always carries the kind of its holder (ledger:claimKind, stamped by the host). An expired lease is observable and never silently free." .

ledger:claimedAt a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "claimedAt" ;
    rdfs:comment "When the current claim was taken or last renewed, from the kernel clock; on a ledger:ReleaseEvent, that time for the claim it released. No domain since 0.15.0: an event must not become an inferred Item." .

ledger:purpose a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "purpose" ;
    rdfs:comment "Why the holder took the claim — kata's `purpose` column, and our brief name. Free text, for a human reading `items`." .

ledger:onItem a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:label "onItem" ;
    rdfs:comment "The item a comment or StatusEvent belongs to. The edge points from the owned node so appending it is a pure INSERT; deletion includes every node with this edge. No domain: an event must not become an inferred Comment." .

# ---- links between items ----------------------------------------------------
# Typed predicates rather than a link node with a type column: an edge is one
# triple, the ready query is one NOT EXISTS, and cycle detection walks the
# predicate directly. kata's three link types, kept.

ledger:blocks a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:range ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:label "blocks" ;
    rdfs:comment "This item must finish before the object can start. ⚠ These edges are meant to be a DAG and NOTHING ENFORCES IT: a cycle makes the ready set silently empty, which is indistinguishable from a finished backlog. urn:iki:ledger:next walks the open subgraph by hand and REFUSES with the cycle named rather than answering \"nothing is ready\"." .

ledger:parent a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:range ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:label "parent" ;
    rdfs:comment "This item is part of that one. Structural only — it does not block, and the ready set ignores it." .

ledger:related a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:range ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:label "related" ;
    rdfs:comment "See also. Symmetric in intent; written one way and queried both." .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Deletion
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

ledger:deletedItem a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Tombstone ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Resource ;
    rdfs:label "deletedItem" ;
    rdfs:comment "The IRI that was deleted. It no longer resolves to an item; it resolves to this." .

ledger:recoverable a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Tombstone ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "recoverable" ;
    rdfs:comment "true: the item's quads were MOVED to the quarantine graph urn:iki:ledger:graph:{ledger}:deleted and can be brought back. false: they were destroyed — by a purge of the live item, or by a later purge of what the delete archived — and this tombstone plus its sig:contentHash is all the evidence there is." .

ledger:quadCount a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Tombstone ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "quadCount" ;
    rdfs:comment "How many quads the delete removed — the size of what is gone, which the hash alone does not say." .

ledger:purgedAt a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Tombstone ;
    rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "purgedAt" ;
    rdfs:comment "When the content was destroyed. Present exactly when ledger:recoverable is false. On a tombstone a purge wrote directly it equals prov:invalidatedAtTime; on one a delete wrote first, it is the later moment the archive was destroyed, and the delete's own ledger:reason, ledger:author, ledger:quadCount and sig:contentHash stay as the delete wrote them." .

ledger:purgeReason a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Tombstone ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "purgeReason" ;
    rdfs:comment "Why an already-deleted item's archive was destroyed — kept apart from ledger:reason, which says why it was deleted, because the two are different decisions, often by different people." .

ledger:purgedBy a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Tombstone ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "purgedBy" ;
    rdfs:comment "Who destroyed an already-deleted item's archive, as a plain name (see ledger:author for why a string)." .

ledger:reason a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "reason" ;
    rdfs:comment "Free text: why an item was deleted, or why a candidate was excluded from a selection." .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Selection
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

ledger:lastNumber a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Counter ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "lastNumber" .

ledger:policy a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Selection ;
    rdfs:range ledger:Policy ;
    rdfs:label "policy" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which policy produced this answer. Being able to SAY which policy ranked this matters more than having several policies." .

ledger:weighs a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Policy ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "weighs" ;
    rdfs:comment "One signal this policy uses, in the order it uses them. Meta on the policy resource says the same thing in prose." .

ledger:graph a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Ledger ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Resource ;
    rdfs:label "graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "The named graph a ledger's quads live in. Stated rather than left implicit, so a consumer can go straight to urn:iki:store:select over one ledger without reconstructing the IRI from the name." .

ledger:itemCount a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Ledger ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "itemCount" ;
    rdfs:comment "How many items the ledger holds, open and closed together." .

ledger:openCount a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "openCount" ;
    rdfs:comment "How many items are open, on a ledger summary or a DailyCounts observation. No domain: an observation must not become an inferred Ledger." .

ledger:readyCount a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Selection ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "readyCount" ;
    rdfs:comment "How many items the deterministic ready query returned — the narrowing, before any policy ran." .

ledger:excludedCount a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Selection ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "excludedCount" .

ledger:ranking a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Selection ;
    rdfs:range ledger:Ranking ;
    rdfs:label "ranking" .

ledger:excluded a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Selection ;
    rdfs:range ledger:Exclusion ;
    rdfs:label "excluded" .

ledger:rank a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Ranking ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "rank" ;
    rdfs:comment "1 is the answer. The rest are what it beat, in order — a ranking that cannot be interrogated gets overridden and then ignored." .

ledger:item a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:label "item" ;
    rdfs:comment "The item a ranking or an exclusion is about. No domain: both classes use it." .

ledger:score a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Ranking ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "score" ;
    rdfs:comment "The policy's own score, as it computed it. A string because a score is only comparable WITHIN one policy — publishing it as a number invites comparison across policies, which means nothing." .

ledger:because a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Ranking ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "because" ;
    rdfs:comment "One reason, in the policy's words, for where this item landed. Several per ranking." .

ledger:leverage a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Ranking ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "leverage" ;
    rdfs:comment "How many OPEN items this one blocks, transitively. It falls out of the link graph for free and is usually the best single signal, because finishing a blocker converts several unready items into ready ones — and a priority sort cannot see it at all." .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Lifecycles: the one state an item is in, and the vocabulary that says which are legal
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

ledger:Lifecycle a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Lifecycle" ;
    rdfs:comment "The legal states an item moves through, in order, as a resource at urn:iki:ledger:lifecycle:{name}: which are IN FLIGHT (worked under a claim) and, for every other state, the DRAIN that owns an item parked there. Host configuration like a ledger:Policy, but fixed per ledger rather than chosen per request: a policy only orders an answer, while a lifecycle decides which values a state may hold. ★ An item holding no ledger:state is in the state named `filed`, which every lifecycle declares, so absence is a state with an owner rather than a residue." .

ledger:LifecycleState a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "LifecycleState" ;
    rdfs:comment "One state of a ledger:Lifecycle, named {lifecycle IRI}:{name} so the IRI a transition writes is the one the lifecycle declares. rdfs:label is its name." .

ledger:hasState a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Lifecycle ;
    rdfs:range ledger:LifecycleState ;
    rdfs:label "hasState" ;
    rdfs:comment "A state of this lifecycle. The states' ledger:order puts them in sequence." .

ledger:order a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:LifecycleState ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "order" ;
    rdfs:comment "A state's position in its lifecycle; `filed` is first. Unique within one lifecycle." .

ledger:inFlight a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:LifecycleState ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "inFlight" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether an item in this state is being worked, and so should be held by a claim. The doctor reports an in-flight item with no claim (abandoned) and a machine claim on an item that is not in flight (orphaned)." .

ledger:drain a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:LifecycleState ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "drain" ;
    rdfs:comment "Who picks an item up from this state: present exactly when the state is not in flight. The contract's rule that nothing parks outside the flight without saying who owns it, made a required property of the vocabulary." .

ledger:movesTo a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:LifecycleState ;
    rdfs:range ledger:LifecycleState ;
    rdfs:label "movesTo" ;
    rdfs:comment "A state of the same lifecycle an item may go to from this one; repeatable. When a lifecycle declares any, urn:iki:ledger:{ledger}:item:{id}:state refuses a move it does not declare, and a state with none is one an item cannot leave. When it declares none, any state may move to any other. A state does not declare a move to itself: a request to stay changes nothing." .

ledger:state a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:range ledger:LifecycleState ;
    rdfs:label "state" ;
    rdfs:comment "The ONE lifecycle state this item is in; absent means `filed`. Written only by urn:iki:ledger:{ledger}:item:{id}:state, as a compare-and-set in a single store update, so an interrupted transition leaves the old state or the new one and never both. ⚠ Two values can only come from an out-of-band write, and the doctor reports them." .

ledger:stateAt a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "stateAt" ;
    rdfs:comment "When the item entered its current state, from the kernel's clock." .

ledger:stateToken a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "stateToken" ;
    rdfs:comment "An opaque id minted for each transition and written by it: the receipt the compare-and-set reads back to learn whether ITS update was the one that applied. Two transitions racing from one state to the same state would otherwise both read back the state they asked for and both report success." .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Claims: who holds an item (as the host says), and for how long
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

ledger:ClaimKind a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "ClaimKind" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether a claim is held by a machine, an agent or a person. ★ STAMPED BY THE HOST, never taken from the caller: the doctor reports a machine claim on an item that is not in flight and leaves a person's hold alone, so a kind the caller could choose would let a machine pose as a person to escape it. A claim from before ledger:claimKind existed is read as a machine's." .

ledger:machine a ledger:ClaimKind ;
    rdfs:label "machine" ;
    rdfs:comment "A claim held by a loop, a client or a service. The kind a host that does not decide stamps on every claim." .

ledger:agent a ledger:ClaimKind ;
    rdfs:label "agent" ;
    rdfs:comment "A claim held by an LLM agent, such as a collaborator's Claude. Reported by the doctor as a machine's is when the item is not in flight: an agent acting for a person (prov:actedOnBehalfOf) is still stamped agent, because the hold is the agent's. Since 0.11.0." .

ledger:person a ledger:ClaimKind ;
    rdfs:label "person" ;
    rdfs:comment "A claim held by a person, deliberately." .

ledger:claimKind a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range ledger:ClaimKind ;
    rdfs:label "claimKind" ;
    rdfs:comment "Who holds this item's claim: ledger:machine, ledger:agent or ledger:person, as the host stamped it when the claim was taken. Also on a ledger:ClaimEvent and a ledger:ReleaseEvent, for the claim each records; no domain since 0.15.0, so an event is not an inferred Item." .

ledger:lease a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:range xsd:duration ;
    rdfs:label "lease" ;
    rdfs:comment "How long the claim was taken for. Absent for a claim held until it is released." .

ledger:leaseExpires a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "leaseExpires" ;
    rdfs:comment "When the claim's lease runs out, from the kernel's clock. ⚠ An expired lease is NOT a free item: the claim stays, `next` excludes the item as lease-expired naming the holder and this time, and only `claim takeover=true from=<holder>` takes it — a compare-and-set against that holder. A lease that expired silently would make \"owned\" mean \"owned as of an unspecified time\"." .

ledger:claimPrincipal a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Resource ;
    rdfs:label "claimPrincipal" ;
    rdfs:comment "The principal the claim was taken AS: the one `urn:cap:principal:<iri>` the claimant's capability carried, minted by the host's door (gonk's QUIC door names each client `urn:iki:gonk:client:<fingerprint>`). Recorded beside ledger:claimedBy, which stays the holder's free-text label. ★ This, not the label, is what a release checks: a claim recorded with a principal is released only by a capability that acts as it, or by a dispatcher holding the ledger's close or write grant, so one worker cannot release another's claim by spelling its name. Absent when the claimant carried no principal (a host without them, or root), and then a holder is free text as it always was. Also on a ledger:ClaimEvent and a ledger:ReleaseEvent, for the claim each records; no domain since 0.15.0, so an event is not an inferred Item." .

ledger:awaiting a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Resource ;
    rdfs:label "awaiting" ;
    rdfs:comment "Who an open item waits on: <urn:iki:ledger:awaiting:dispatcher> after a worker hands it back (`comment handback=true`, under the comment grant, in the same update as the comment). `next` does not offer an awaiting item. Cleared by close, by reopen, by a later comment with `rejects=true`, and by any comment from a caller holding the ledger's close grant. A state rather than a comment-text convention, because a worker cannot label (that needs the write grant) and `next` cannot read prose." .

<urn:iki:ledger:awaiting:dispatcher>
    rdfs:label "dispatcher" ;
    rdfs:comment "The value of ledger:awaiting for an item handed back to whoever dispatched it, to check the evidence and close it, or reject it." .

ledger:awaitingAt a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "awaiting decision at" ;
    rdfs:comment "The kernel time of the latest hand-back decision (since 0.13.1). Written atomically with handback=true, rejects=true, a dispatcher comment, close or reopen. While awaiting the dispatcher, this is when the hand-back set that state; ordinary worker comments do not reset it. Retained when cleared so the doctor does not mistake older legacy Evidence: comments for unanswered hand-backs." .

ledger:closedAt a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:comment "When the current close occurred, from the kernel clock. Removed on reopen; dated StatusEvent nodes retain subsequent history. Legacy values supplied by backfill are approximate and marked closedAtSource backfill." .
ledger:closedAtSource a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment "Where a close time came from, when it is not the kernel clock. The literal backfill marks an approximate legacy close time: present on the item and its backfilled StatusEvent, which retains this provenance after reopen, and DailyCounts observations through the latest estimated close day also carry it. The literal import (since 0.15.0, ledger #2819) marks a close date an import principal (ledger:Importer) claimed with closed-at=, on the item and on the StatusEvent dated by it; the StatusEvent's ledger:committedAt keeps the true time of the write." .
ledger:StatusEvent a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "A dated close or reopen: onItem names its item, status its resulting status, and dcterms:created its kernel time (or, marked closedAtSource import, the source date an import principal claimed). Since 0.15.0 it carries ledger:committer and ledger:committedAt, so it says who closed or reopened, not only when. Since 0.18.0 a close also carries ledger:closedReason and any ledger:evidence. Skolemized beneath the item. Retained across reopen so historical daily counts remain true." .

ledger:evidence a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "evidence" ;
    rdfs:comment "What closed an item, on that close's StatusEvent, written in the same update as the close and kept there across a reopen (ledger #2842). Repeatable. Its role is read from where it hangs and the target's kind: a commit on a done close is the fix, an item on a duplicate or superseded close is its target, a test is the proof. An item was resolved when the close was made; any other IRI is stored as given and never fetched. History, not an edge: a delete of the item it names leaves it, as it leaves ledger:editTarget." .

# ---- attribution events (ledger #2819) ---------------------------------------
# A claim is current state, retracted by its release; these record who claimed,
# released and edited each item, so "what did this agent do" is a query after the
# claims are gone. Each is written in the same store update as the change it records,
# under the same guard, so a write that loses its race leaves no event. Each hangs off
# its item by ledger:onItem, so delete, archive and purge own it like a StatusEvent, and
# each is skolemized beneath its item: {item}:claim:{id}, {item}:release:{id},
# {item}:edit:{id}. Each carries dcterms:created, ledger:committedAt and, when the
# capability carried a principal, ledger:committer.

ledger:ClaimEvent a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "claim event" ;
    rdfs:comment "A claim taken: a first claim of a free item, or a takeover of an expired lease, never a renewal. Carries ledger:claimedBy (the holder label), ledger:claimPrincipal (when the claimant carried one), ledger:claimKind, and ledger:takeoverFrom on a takeover. Concurrent claims of one free item leave one." .

ledger:ReleaseEvent a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "release event" ;
    rdfs:comment "A claim released, written only when there was a claim to release. Carries the released claim's ledger:claimedBy, ledger:claimPrincipal, ledger:claimKind and ledger:claimedAt (each when it had one), ledger:releaseReason, and the releaser as ledger:committer: the holder, a dispatcher's override, the closer, or the taker." .

ledger:EditEvent a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "edit event" ;
    rdfs:comment "An edit that creates no node of its own, so that every write has a who: one per write, naming the edit in ledger:editKind, its maker in ledger:committer and, when it changed something, what it changed (ledger:editLabel, ledger:stateFrom and ledger:stateTo, ledger:editLinkType and ledger:editTarget, ledger:editField). A claim, a release and a close or reopen are recorded by their own events instead." .

ledger:takeoverFrom a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "takeover from" ;
    rdfs:comment "On a ledger:ClaimEvent, the holder label a takeover took the expired claim from (the claim's from=). Absent on a first claim." .

ledger:releaseReason a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "release reason" ;
    rdfs:comment "Why a ledger:ReleaseEvent's claim ended, as a literal: released (a release, by the holder or a dispatcher), closed (a close releases the claim), or takeover (a takeover replaced it)." .

ledger:handedBackBy a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "handed back by" ;
    rdfs:comment "On a hand-back comment (comment handback=true), the holder label of the item's claim when the hand-back landed, written in the same update. The comment's ledger:committer is the principal, which two workers can share; this label tells them apart. Absent when nobody held the item." .

ledger:editKind a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "edit kind" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which edit a ledger:EditEvent records, as a literal: label, unlabel, link, unlink, defer, resume, about, unabout, edit (an item's title, body, priority, revision or about) or state (a lifecycle transition)." .
# What an edit changed (ledger #2841): each is written by the update that makes the change, only
# when it changes something, so a write that changed nothing has an event that names no object.

ledger:stateFrom a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "state from" ;
    rdfs:comment "On a ledger:EditEvent of kind state, the IRI of the lifecycle state the item left, as the transition's own update found it, the IRI of filed when it held none." .

ledger:stateTo a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "state to" ;
    rdfs:comment "On a ledger:EditEvent of kind state, the IRI of the lifecycle state the item moved to." .

ledger:lifecycleVersion a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "lifecycle version" ;
    rdfs:comment "On a ledger:EditEvent of kind state, the version of the lifecycle document the move was checked against, written by the update that makes the move; an event written before 0.18.0 holds none. On a ledger:Lifecycle whose document has no owl:versionInfo, that document's version. The term has no rdfs:domain because both carry it." .

ledger:editLabel a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "edit label" ;
    rdfs:comment "On a ledger:EditEvent of kind label or unlabel, the label added or removed." .

ledger:editTarget a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "edit target" ;
    rdfs:comment "On a ledger:EditEvent of kind link or unlink, the other item's IRI, possibly in another ledger; of kind about or unabout, each target IRI added or removed. It is history, not an edge: it says what the edit named then, and stays when the item it names is deleted." .

ledger:editLinkType a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "edit link type" ;
    rdfs:comment "On a ledger:EditEvent of kind link or unlink, the type of the link, as a link predicate (ledger:blocks, ledger:parent, ledger:related)." .

ledger:editField a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "edit field" ;
    rdfs:comment "On a ledger:EditEvent of kind edit, the name of a field the edit changed: title, body, priority, revision or about. Never its value." .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Typed links across ledgers (ledger #2829)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

ledger:Link a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "cross-ledger link" ;
    rdfs:comment "An edge from an item to an item of ANOTHER ledger, written in the subject's graph: ledger:onItem the subject, ledger:linkType, ledger:linkTarget, with ledger:committer and ledger:committedAt. Admitted by write on the subject's ledger and urn:cap:ledger:link-in on the target's. An edge within one ledger stays a plain ledger:blocks, ledger:parent or ledger:related triple. Skolemized as {subject}:link:{type}:{target ledger}:{target id}." .

ledger:Mirror a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "cross-ledger mirror" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the target's graph knows of a ledger:Link of type blocks or parent: ledger:onItem the target, ledger:linkType, ledger:mirrorOf the subject's IRI, ledger:subjectOpen, ledger:linkPresent and ledger:mirrorVersion, and nothing of the subject's content. Written by the ledger after every link, unlink, close and reopen of the subject, only when its version is at least the one it holds, so the last change of the subject wins whatever order the writes land in. Skolemized as {target}:mirror:{type}:{subject ledger}:{subject id}." .

ledger:linkType a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "link type" ;
    rdfs:comment "The edge a ledger:Link or a ledger:Mirror is: ledger:blocks, ledger:parent or ledger:related, as an IRI." .

ledger:linkTarget a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Link ;
    rdfs:range ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:label "link target" ;
    rdfs:comment "The item, in another ledger, a ledger:Link points at." .

ledger:mirrorOf a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Mirror ;
    rdfs:range ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:label "mirror of" ;
    rdfs:comment "The subject, in another ledger, whose edge a ledger:Mirror reflects. Its IRI only." .

ledger:subjectOpen a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Mirror ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "subject open" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether the subject was open at the version the mirror was written from. An open blocker keeps the target out of next." .

ledger:linkPresent a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Mirror ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "link present" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether the edge still exists. An unlink leaves the mirror with false rather than removing it, so a late write from an older version cannot bring the edge back." .

ledger:mirrorVersion a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Mirror ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "mirror version" ;
    rdfs:comment "How many ledger:StatusEvent and ledger:EditEvent nodes the subject had when the mirror was read from it. Every close, reopen, link and unlink adds one inside the update that makes the change, so the count only grows." .

ledger:DailyCounts a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "One UTC end-of-day observation, skolemized as urn:iki:ledger:{name}:counts:{date}. The current day's row is provisional. openCount plus closedCount is the number filed by that date." .
ledger:date a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:date ;
    rdfs:comment "The UTC calendar date of a daily counts observation." .
ledger:closedCount a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:comment "Items filed by this date whose latest recorded status at the end of the UTC day is closed." .

ledger:eventSequence a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:comment "The graph-local ordering of an item's status events, disambiguating transitions at the same kernel-clock millisecond. Backfill starts at zero." .

# ---- aliases (ledger #2828) ----------------------------------------------------
# Written in ONE deployment-wide graph, urn:iki:alias:graph, never in a ledger's graph:
# an alias is unique per scheme across every ledger, and uniqueness is decided by one
# update over one graph. Every node is skolemized: urn:iki:alias:scheme:{scheme} for a
# scheme and urn:iki:alias:scheme:{scheme}:value:{value} for an alias, the value
# percent-encoded outside the unreserved characters.

ledger:AliasScheme a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "alias scheme" ;
    rdfs:comment "A namespace of external ids (kata, roborev, gh), registered once with the one principal that may add aliases in it, ledger:schemeOwner. Carries ledger:schemeName, ledger:committer and ledger:committedAt. Skolemized as urn:iki:alias:scheme:{scheme}." .

ledger:Alias a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "alias" ;
    rdfs:comment "One (scheme, value) pair naming one item: ledger:aliasScheme, ledger:aliasValue and ledger:aliasOf, with ledger:committer and ledger:committedAt. Unique per scheme across the deployment, so an alias already naming another item is refused. Not a replacement for ledger:key, the idempotency key, and an item's primary name stays its number. Skolemized as urn:iki:alias:scheme:{scheme}:value:{value}." .

ledger:schemeName a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:AliasScheme ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "scheme name" ;
    rdfs:comment "A scheme's name as it is typed in an alias reference, {scheme}:{value}: lowercase letters, digits and -, starting with a letter." .

ledger:schemeOwner a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:AliasScheme ;
    rdfs:label "scheme owner" ;
    rdfs:comment "The principal (an IRI, the urn:cap:principal: its capability carried) that registered the scheme and the only one that may add or remove aliases in it." .

ledger:aliasScheme a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Alias ;
    rdfs:range ledger:AliasScheme ;
    rdfs:label "alias scheme" ;
    rdfs:comment "The scheme an alias is in." .

ledger:aliasValue a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Alias ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "alias value" ;
    rdfs:comment "An alias's value within its scheme, exactly as given (decoded): acme/web#12 in gh:acme/web#12." .

ledger:aliasOf a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain ledger:Alias ;
    rdfs:range ledger:Item ;
    rdfs:label "alias of" ;
    rdfs:comment "The item an alias names. Exactly one per alias, decided inside the write that adds it." .
